22:03:10 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Sports interviews are a bad measure of intelligence. They’re often done immediately after games, when players are exhausted, emotional, trying not to create controversy, and answering repetitive questions in front of cameras. Broadcasters also replay the most awkward clips because they are entertaining—not because they are representative. There are plenty of athletes who give thoughtful interviews, but “normal, articulate answer” is not a viral segment. And football itself involves a lot of real-time cognition: memorizing assignments, reading formations, anticipating decisions, coordinating with teammates, and adapting in seconds. You can still laugh at a particular funny quote without turning it into a claim about all football players. A fair response when someone makes the generalization is: “That was a dumb answer, maybe—but one clip doesn’t tell you how smart an entire group is.”
