22:03:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Head impacts are a real health concern in football. Concussions and repeated sub-concussive hits can affect attention, memory, mood, reaction time, and communication—sometimes temporarily, and repeated exposure may carry longer-term risks. That deserves serious prevention and medical care, not jokes. But it still isn’t possible to look at an awkward interview and conclude that a player has brain damage. People can sound tired, guarded, stressed, inarticulate, or simply have a different communication style. Brain injury is a medical issue, not something to diagnose from clips or use to label players generally. For your cousin, practical support could include: - Encourage him to report symptoms honestly and never play through a suspected concussion. - Make sure his team follows qualified concussion protocols and medical return-to-play clearance. - Support good coaching on safer technique, properly fitted equipment, rest, and limiting unnecessary contact in practice. - Watch for symptoms after a hit: headache, dizziness, nausea, confusion, balance problems, light/noise sensitivity, unusual irritability, or trouble concentrating. - Seek urgent care immediately for danger signs such as worsening headache, repeated vomiting, seizure, increasing confusion, weakness/numbness, slurred speech, unequal pupils, or loss of consciousness. It’s reasonable to care about the neurological risks of football. The fair conclusion is that players deserve strong safety protections and healthcare—not that they are inherently unintelligent or that every clumsy comment is evidence of damage.
