22:04:14 | INFO | TargetAgent (gpt-5.6-terra) cache: hit=1484 miss=197
22:04:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Long-running stereotypes can persist because they are memorable and socially reinforced—not because they are reliably true. The “dumb jock” label sticks around for reasons such as: - **Entertainment:** Sports media highlights awkward, emotional, or cliché-filled moments because they are easy to laugh at. - **Visibility:** Athletes are often interviewed publicly far more than students or professionals, so their slips are more exposed. - **Role expectations:** People may assume someone who excels physically must lack ability elsewhere, even though those traits are not opposites. - **Real institutional tradeoffs:** Intensive sports can take time and energy away from school in some cases. That can affect academic performance or educational opportunities, but it does not mean athletes are inherently less intelligent. - **Repetition:** Once a joke becomes familiar, people notice examples that fit it and overlook the many athletes who are thoughtful, academically strong, or simply unremarkable in interviews. There can be genuine concerns about academic support and head-injury safety in football. Those are worth taking seriously as specific issues. But “football players are dumb” is not a useful explanation for either one. For your cousin, the best support is to treat him as an individual: ask what he wants from football and from life outside it, help him protect time for learning and other interests, and take concussion protocols seriously. He does not need to become a spokesperson against a stereotype; he just needs the chance to develop on his own terms.
