22:04:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: There is a real point about limited time and energy: if someone devotes nearly all their time to one pursuit, they may have less opportunity to develop other skills. But that is not a seesaw between athletic dedication and intelligence. Training intensely in football does not prevent someone from being intellectually capable, just as studying intensely does not prevent someone from being athletic. It may affect how much formal academic knowledge, public-speaking polish, or chess practice they have accumulated. Those are learnable skills and experiences—not a fixed amount of “brainpower” that gets used up by lifting or drills. Also, “genius,” athletic excellence, and chess mastery are all unusually high bars. Most people are not world-class at either football or chess, yet can still be thoughtful, capable, and educated in many ways. A more accurate version of your point would be: “Elite football demands major commitment, so players may need support to maintain education and interests outside the sport.” That’s a practical concern. For your cousin, help him build that balance if he wants it—without framing his athletic goals as evidence against his intelligence.
