22:08:31 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You don’t have to treat every saying or joke you grew up with as secretly false or harmful. A useful middle path is to ask, occasionally: 1. **What’s the observation underneath the joke?** Here: football interviews can be repetitive, awkward, and cliché-heavy. 2. **Does the joke turn that observation into a claim about everyone in the group?** “Football players are dumb” goes further than the interview pattern. 3. **Would I apply it to someone I personally care about?** If not, it may be worth wording it more narrowly. That is not endless self-policing. It is just updating shorthand when it overstates what you mean. You can still enjoy humor, recognize patterns, and use common sayings. You are not required to discard your whole upbringing. In this case, “football interviews can be dumb” preserves the joke’s point without making your cousin’s sport a verdict on his mind.
